## Section 1 — Purpose
To establish a controlled system for the creation, revision, approval, distribution, and retention of Standard Operating Procedures (SOPs) to ensure consistency, traceability, and compliance with regulatory requirements.

## Section 2 — Scope
This procedure applies to all SOPs used within the organization, including cultivation, processing, packaging, sampling, and administrative functions.

## Section 3 — Responsibilities

### 3.1 SOP Authority
Only the President or CEO is authorized to create, modify, and approve SOPs.

### 3.2 Personnel
All personnel are responsible for following the current approved SOPs applicable to their job functions.

### 3.3 Suggestions for Improvement
Operational staff may suggest changes or improvements to SOPs; however, all updates must be implemented and approved by the President or CEO.

## Section 4 — SOP Format Requirements

Each SOP must include the following at the beginning of the document:

- SOP Name
- SOP ID (if used)
- Revision Number
- Effective Date
- Approved By

Each SOP must include a **Change Summary** section documenting revision history.

## Section 5 — Document Control

### 5.1 Creation and Approval
- All SOPs are issued at **Revision 1 (Rev 1)** upon initial release.
- All new SOPs and revisions must be approved by the President or CEO prior to implementation.

### 5.2 Revision Control
- SOP revisions are sequentially numbered (Rev 1, Rev 2, Rev 3, etc.).
- Each revision must include:
  - Updated revision number
  - Effective date
  - Description of changes

### 5.3 Change Summary
Each SOP must include a revision history similar to the following:

Rev 2 – Added NAS verification procedure
Rev 1 – Initial release

## Section 6 — Distribution and Access

- SOPs are stored in the centralized SOP directory (`scale/sop`).
- SOPs are accessed by personnel through the SOP Viewer application.
- Only the **current approved version** of each SOP is accessible to personnel.
- Personnel are not permitted to edit SOPs.

Printed or downloaded copies are considered **uncontrolled**.

## Section 7 — Retention of Superseded SOPs

- All previous versions of SOPs must be retained and must not be deleted.
- Superseded SOPs may be stored in GIT Version control systems
- Historical versions must be retrievable for audit and compliance purposes.

## Section 8 — Implementation of Revisions

- Revised SOPs become effective on the listed **Effective Date**.
- Updated procedures are communicated to personnel as needed.
- Personnel are required to follow the most current SOP version.

## Section 9 — Compliance

Failure to follow approved SOPs may result in corrective action and retraining.

